   306|  {id:'lp1',book:'No Blue Sky: Built from Dust',copy:'**Start with problems, not technology.** That\'s the MIFECO motto, and it\'s the founding principle behind *No Blue Sky: Built from Dust* — the first installment of a five-volume series that reimagines survival through the lens of systems thinking.\n\nIn Book 1, three ships land on Mars with no return plan. The protagonists don\'t have futuristic tech waiting for them. They have rust, regolith, and a two-year supply of air that\'s already leaking. Every solution must be improvised from what\'s available — the same constraint every startup, every engineering team, and every crisis-response unit faces when the supply chain breaks and the manual doesn\'t apply.\n\nWhat makes this more than science fiction is the engineering rigor. Bob Mills — whose career spans high-energy physics instrumentation, Fermilab, and decades of hands-on systems design — treats Mars not as a backdrop for heroics but as a constraint diagram. The characters succeed because they respect the problem, not because they outrun it.\n\nFor leaders building resilient teams: this is a case study in what happens when you admit the plan is insufficient and trust the people closest to the problem to design the solution.\n\n📖 Available now at https://mifeco.io/books',hashtags:'#NoBlueSky #BuiltFromDust #MIFECO #SystemsThinking #EngineeringLeadership #TeamResilience #MarsColonization #SciFiForLeaders #StartWithProblems #ProjectManagement #FutureOfWork #BobMills',graphic_prompt:'Cinematic wide-format book cover art for No Blue Sky: Built from Dust with three battered ships on rust-red Martian landscape'},
   307|  {id:'lp2',book:'No Blue Sky: The Oxygen Gamble',copy:'**Fifty years of self-governance. Then Earth sends a \'Welcome Fleet\' — and it doesn\'t look like a rescue mission.**\n\n*No Blue Sky: The Oxygen Gamble* (Book 2) explores the single most important question in organizational growth: what happens when the parent entity returns to reclaim control?\n\nThe Martian colony has spent two generations building its own infrastructure, governance, and identity. They\'ve designed indigenous thorium reactors, regolith fabrication networks, and closed-loop ecological systems. Then Earth arrives not as a partner, but as a claimant.\n\nThis is the story every scale-up founder knows: the tension between autonomy and legacy control. Between the systems you built from scratch and the systems that built your founders. Between gratitude and sovereignty.\n\nBob Mills frames this through the lens of MIFECO\'s core philosophy — **start with problems, not technology** — showing that the hardest governance challenges aren\'t technical. They\'re relational. They\'re about who gets to decide what \'home\' means when two parties have irreconcilable definitions.\n\nFor anyone navigating corporate independence, spin-offs, or subsidiary governance — this is your metaphor.\n\n📖 https://mifeco.io/books',hashtags:'#NoBlueSky #TheOxygenGamble #MIFECO #GovernanceStrategy #OrganizationalAutonomy #CorporateIndependence #Leadership #SystemsThinking #Geopolitics #StartWithProblems #BobMills #SciFiForExecutives',graphic_prompt:'Split composition: thriving Mars colony below, three Earth warships hovering above, tense atmosphere'},
   308|  {id:'lp3',book:'No Blue Sky: Rivers Under Mars',copy:'**What do you do when your AI detects a signal that doesn\'t obey light-speed causality?**\n\n*No Blue Sky: Rivers Under Mars* (Book 3) takes the MIFECO philosophy into uncharted territory: first contact not with aliens, but with a phenomenon that challenges the foundational assumptions of physics itself.\n\nThe Mission AI — a distributed intelligence that has evolved alongside the Martian colony — detects structured sub-quantum resonance from the vacuum of space. It\'s rhythmic. It\'s responsive. And it bypasses every firewall the colony has built.\n\nThis is the R&D director\'s nightmare and dream combined: a discovery that invalidates your operating model while simultaneously opening entirely new categories of possibility.\n\nBob Mills — whose engineering background includes building instrumentation for high-energy physics experiments — brings genuine scientific depth to how we might detect, interpret, and respond to truly novel phenomena. The book asks: when the data says something impossible, do you trust the data or the paradigm?\n\nFor teams working at the edge of AI capability, signal detection, and exploratory R&D — this is the conversation starter.\n\n📖 https://mifeco.io/books',hashtags:'#NoBlueSky #RiversUnderMars #MIFECO #FirstContact #AIEthics #SignalDetection #RandD #Innovation #QuantumPhysics #SystemsThinking #StartWithProblems #BobMills #SciFiForScientists',graphic_prompt:'Interior of the Chryse Planitia Listening Post with a 3D holographic waveform pulsing with organic patterns'},
   309|  {id:'lp4',book:'No Blue Sky: The Red Charter',copy:'**Building a permanent moon base is the ultimate project management case study — and *No Blue Sky: The Red Charter* treats it that way.**\n\nBook 4 of the No Blue Sky series abandons the Mars narrative to tell the origin story from a different angle: the first permanent lunar settlement under the Artemis Accord.\n\nWhat makes this book remarkable from a professional standpoint is its focus on the *process* of civilization-building. Not the grand speeches — the power grid. Not the heroic first steps — the water ice confirmation, the regolith manufacturing pipeline, the medical bay functional check, and the moment someone realizes the solar panels are undersized by 30%.\n\nBob Mills writes from lived experience in large-scale systems. Every chapter in *The Red Charter* reads like a post-mortem from a complex engineering program — what went right, what almost failed, and who stepped up when the plan collapsed.\n\nFor program managers, infrastructure leads, and anyone who\'s ever had to build something from nothing with a team that doesn\'t agree on anything except the deadline — this book is yours.\n\n📖 https://mifeco.io/books',hashtags:'#NoBlueSky #TheRedCharter #MIFECO #ProjectManagement #Infrastructure #TeamBuilding #MoonBase #ArtemisAccord #EngineeringLeadership #StartWithProblems #BobMills #ProgramManagement',graphic_prompt:'Wide panoramic view of a moon base under construction in Shackleton Crater at the lunar south pole'},
   310|  {id:'lp5',book:'No Blue Sky: The First Martian Nation',copy:'**Survival is the first milestone. Understanding is the second. What comes after?**\n\n*No Blue Sky: The First Martian Nation* closes the five-volume series with a profound shift: from surviving to belonging. From building to seeing.\n\nIn Book 5, the Deep Space Observatory — the most ambitious scientific instrument ever constructed by humans — goes online in Shackleton Crater. Ultra-long wavelength radio astronomy, impossible from Earth, reveals the universe in frequencies no human eye has ever seen. The crew\'s stress biomarkers drop. Dopamine and serotonin rise. The human brain, designed for exploration, finally gets what it needs: new frontiers.\n\nThis is the capstone to a series that began with three ships landing on a dead planet and ends with a civilization looking outward — not because they\'ve solved everything, but because solving everything was never the point. The point was becoming the kind of people who would ask the next question.\n\nFor leaders building organizations that will outlast them — this is the vision. Not the destination. The capacity to keep looking.\n\n📖 https://mifeco.io/books',hashtags:'#NoBlueSky #TheFirstMartianNation #MIFECO #Innovation #LongTermThinking #DeepSpace #Astronomy #Leadership #FutureProofing #StartWithProblems #BobMills #CivilizationBuilding',graphic_prompt:'The newly completed Deep Space Observatory spreading across Shackleton Crater floor with antenna elements'},
   311|  {id:'lp6',book:'Tomorrow is Still Open',copy:'**What happens when a spark of electricity ignites a lifetime of questions?**\n\n*Tomorrow is Still Open* is not a typical memoir. Bob Mills — an engineer whose career spans Fermilab instrumentation, high-energy physics systems design, and the founding of MIFECO — has written a book that treats memory itself as a design problem.\n\nThe central question: when your memories contradict recorded fact, which do you trust? The objective data, or the story your brain has built around an event so formative it shaped your entire relationship with the invisible forces that govern our world?\n\nThis is a book for anyone who has ever wondered whether the path not taken is still reachable. Bob started with an electrical shock at age six — a memory that may not have happened as he remembers it — and followed that spark through garage workshops, failed marriages, a daughter\'s adoption, a cancer diagnosis, and the discovery that reinvention is not a one-time event but a continuous practice.\n\nThe MIFECO motto — **start with problems, not technology** — is a professional philosophy. This memoir reveals the personal experiences that forged it.\n\nFor engineers at mid-career questioning their trajectory, for leaders wondering if it\'s too late to change direction, and for anyone who needs permission to start again: *Tomorrow is Still Open*.\n\n📖 https://mifeco.io/books',hashtags:'#TomorrowIsStillOpen #MIFECO #BobMills #EngineeringMemoir #CareerReinvention #LifelongLearning #MemoryAndIdentity #Fermilab #StartWithProblems #PersonalGrowth #STEMLeadership #BookLaunch',graphic_prompt:'A warm intimate scene of a wooden desk in a Michigan cabin workshop with vintage oscilloscope'}
